    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the hierarchy for coding atypical dermatological presentations. We're seeing a lot of cases with irregular, mottled pigmentation and unusual textural changes that don't quite fit the standard L-code classifications for common dermatitis. Can you clarify the protocol for deciding between a specific highly-detailed code versus a more general 'unspecified' code when the morphological features of the lesion are atypical or don't follow a standard pattern?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For atypical dermatological presentations with irregular pigmentation and textural changes, a comprehensive evaluation is recommended, including clinical and histopathological assessments. A general 'unspecified' code (Lxx-XX) may suffice when the morphological features don't fit a standard pattern, but a more detailed code (e.g., Lxx-200075) could be considered if specific features suggest a particular etiology or differential diagnoses. In cases where a more precise diagnosis is uncertain, it's essential to consult with a dermatopathologist for further evaluation. A code of 'unspecified' will ensure that the clinical question remains open to further investigation.",
      "source": "target",
